# Scheduled basket rebalance for the Hyperliquid agent.
#
# ==== DO NOT install this file with `crontab scripts/basket.cron` ====
#
# `crontab <file>` REPLACES the entire crontab, and the collection job is
# already installed (collect.cron). Using it here would silently drop the
# 15-minute data collection -- the job recording data that no API can serve
# after the fact, and the one whose absence is noticed months later.
#
# Append this one line instead:
#
#   (crontab -l 2>/dev/null; echo '0 0 */3 * * /home/jaiiye/CodeBuddy/20260913183734/scripts/basket.sh >/dev/null 2>&1') | crontab -
#
# Then confirm both jobs are present (expect 2):
#
#   crontab -l | grep -cE '^\S+ \S+ \S+ \S+ \S+ '
#
# The SHELL / PATH / MAILTO lines below are already in the installed crontab,
# so appending the single job line is enough.
#
# ---- Schedule: 00:00 UTC every 3 days ----
#
# Why a fixed wall-clock time and not "every 72h": cron has no relative
# interval. hold=18 on 4h bars is 72h, so 3 days is the match, and 00:00 UTC
# is a 4h bar boundary. Two consequences:
#
#   * Day-of-month `*/3` restarts on the 1st, so the gap across a month
#     boundary is 1 or 2 days, not 3. The basket does not care -- it reads
#     whatever the signal is when it runs -- but a period can be shorter than
#     72h.
#   * A missed slot is not made up. If the machine is off at 00:00, nothing
#     replays that period; the next run builds from the later signal.
#
# ---- Mode ----
#
# Runs dry-run. Real orders need BASKET_LIVE=1 in the environment, which cron
# does not inherit from an interactive shell -- put it on the job line:
#
#   BASKET_LIVE=1 0 0 */3 * * .../basket.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
#
# Every pass records its mode in logs/basket.log, so "was that period real" is
# answerable from the log rather than from memory.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=""

# min hour day month weekday   command
0 0 */3 * * /home/jaiiye/CodeBuddy/20260913183734/scripts/basket.sh >/dev/null 2>&1
